INT: So all of you children were born there. What about your parents? Where was your father born?

ID: They were born, both of them were born in a-- I was going to say periphery, in the suburbs of a city called Sarajevo in Bosnia. It's still part of Yugoslavia. I mean, that was part of Yugoslavia.

INT: What was your father's name?

ID: Joseph.

When we came to-- when he came to Split, he opened a little store and he called himself Josip which is a local equivalent for Josef or Joseph.

INT: What kind of store?

ID: They had a store of, let's see, what would you call it? It's like textiles, yard, goods, yard goods for clothing. That's what they sold.

INT: What do you know about your father's family?

ID: About my father's home?

INT: About his family. He was not born in Split. What do you know about his family?

ID: OK. The family lived in a suburb of Sarajevo. It's a little town on top of the hill near Sarajevo. It's called Visoko, which stands for high up there.

My grandfather, my father's father, was also called Isaac, or Isak in our Croatian language. And he had six sons and one daughter. The daughter died in her early age before she was a teenager, I think. And the sons lived, all of them, either in Sarajevo or this town called Visoko, which is a suburb. They had-- they were all married and had their families there.

INT: What was your mother's name?

ID: My mother's name was Chana. Hannah is in English. Chana is the local Croatian. And she worked mostly in the store. And we kids grew up at first with a maid. Then when we got a little older, my father was at home.

INT: What do you know about your mother's family?

ID: My mother's name or family?

INT: Her family, what do you know about her family?

ID: They came from Sarajevo or from Visoko to Split and they settled there. [CLEARS THROAT] Excuse me. They settled there, and my grandfather, my mother's father, his name was Haim Kabiljo. He became an-- I guess they call it sexton, like in a synagogue. He took care of local business, keeping it clean, keeping members on track, informed of the events, trying to get a minyan every day, two times. And they were-- they were pretty poor. They had an apartment, which was subsidized by the Jewish community, a very small community of roughly 200 souls.

INT: Do you know why your parents left the area around Sarajevo?

ID: There was a Second World War, and things were not too good for them. They couldn't make a living. So he came to Split. And that's where he established his family. [PAUSES] Why Split, I don't know. There is, I guess beach areas. Everybody liked it for that.

INT: What are some of your earliest memories from before the war?

ID: Some of my earliest views about what?

INT: The earliest memories.

ID: Memories?

INT: Before the war.

ID: OK. My memories go pretty well down when I was like three years old. When I was four years old, my father took us to Visoko to meet his family. Took me and my older sister. [CLEARS THROAT] My older sister didn't care for Visoko, so she didn't meet the rest of the family. She met my mother's side of the family in Sarajevo, the big city, at that time, maybe 100,000 people. 20,000 of them were Jewish. And my sister liked the bright lights. And I stayed with my grandparents in Visoko. I met all the sons of my grandfather, and they were good to me. And we went swimming in the river that passed through, Visoko into Sarajevo. And I remember the train ride. My father got off the train and bought some sandwiches, and threw them to us through the window from outside. And I could never figure out how he got out when the train stopped. But he got off the train unbeknownst to me, and looked like he was living in a little town where he bought the sandwiches where the train stopped. That's-- I remember swimming in the river in Visoko. And all my uncles were good to me, trying to tell me that my sister lost out by not coming to Visoko. They had several fruit trees. Plums were their big, the big thing, I guess. They had about three or four trees with plums, black plums. They were just in season. And I would pick them from the tree and they were delicious. The honey was coming out. That's one of the big memories. Other memories are my uncle had a leather factory, and I would go in there and watch him run the machines. Sometimes he let me with a scrap of leather to play, and try to convert from the skin of the animal into usable skin for shoes. There were umpteen steps. So anyway, that's how they were attentive to me, and that's my good memory. The other memories, when we were corresponding with them. And they was taken to the concentration camp for the-- that was the end of their lives. They all perished.

INT: You're running ahead of me. We'll get to that in a few minutes. You're going ahead of me so far.

ID: OK.

INT: Can you please give me the names of your sisters? Your oldest sister, what was her name?

ID: Yeah, the Sephardic custom was naming your children after your grandchildren, not dead, but alive and dead. If they were dead, you honored your first one. If they're alive, that's OK. So my older sister had the name of my paternal grandfather, the one living in Visoko. My first offspring from my father and my mother was the girl, and she was named after my father's mother, Blanca. Blanca means white in Spanish, also in Ladino. Next was the son, that was I, and he named me after my paternal grandfather. Just to say, the funny things that I had cousins from my father's brother. Each one of them named their son Isak So there was five Isaks in the young generations, and including me. Anyway, that was a Sephardic tradition. So next part was my mother's name, Sara, and my next sister was named Sara. Esther was my younger sister's name, but that was for my father's aunt who never married. She was, I guess, living by herself. And my father wanted to honor her.

INT: What religious traditions did your family observe?

ID: Would you repeat that, please?

INT: Yes. What religious traditions did your family observe?

ID: My family observes Judaic traditions. If it were to compare it to local here to traditions, I guess, would be conservative Orthodoxy. We didn't have too many that you can call reformed and Orthodox, and conservative, and what other things there are here. It was all one. We had one synagogue. Everybody were there. And the services were run in the Sephardic tradition. I guess rabbis graduated from some Sephardic seminary. And so we were observant. We observed kashrut at home and outside, and we didn't go to school on Shabbat. But I, if I remember correctly, Saturday was not a school day. We didn't do anything Friday night, except have a family dinner and sing Jewish songs until it's time to go to sleep, well, Jewish songs and other songs. And I would call us observant.

We didn't eat out in restaurants.

INT: What do you remember about starting school?

ID: What do I remember from?

INT: Starting school.

ID: We had, well I attended regular school. It was [PAUSES] public school. Even the name in Serbo-Croatian said first public, and it says location. And I attended that. By the way, I was super high honor in my class. I went to four grades elementary, and then I went one year to high school. And I was a very good student, except for art. Art was a killer for me. I couldn't do anything. And I remember that vividly.

Teachers liked me. In the first to fourth grade, I was a teacher's pet. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] I was going to call it-- tell you about antisemitism. But there was none that I know of.

INT: I was just going to ask you. [PAUSES] Can you tell us about the relationships between Jews and non-Jews in school and in your community when you were a child?

ID: Can I do what?

INT: Can you tell us about the relationship between the Jews and the non-Jews in school and in your community.

ID: Yes, Split and its surroundings were Croatians or Dalmatians, and that meant they were Roman Catholics. However, from Serbia, a few people were coming either looking for work or I don't know what they were doing. But in my class, there was always three or four Russian Orthodox. We call them Serbo Orthodox. They did not follow Catholic rules. And in public schools over there followed the religion. They had all kinds of displays on the walls and we had a religious hour I think it's once a week. Their priests would come and give a lesson. But the Russian Orthodox kids, and there were three or four in every grade, and Jewish kids, there was one or two of those, we would go outside and play for that hour. When the bell would ring, we knew that it was time to return to class, and we would return. They would get their religious instructions there. As far as Jewish kids were concerned, we would meet in the afternoon in one of the sections of the city, all of us kids. And the rabbi would take over the class. He would have a couple of rows reserved for first and second grade, then a couple rows, third class, and couple of rows for fourth class. And he would teach us religion. However, our family, we knew as much religion as any kids. We were not coached, but instructed, in everything religious by our father who did not go to the store. He stayed home. And he would teach us religious matters.

And when we went to the religious class, the rabbi would kibitz. He said, what am I having you here for? You know more than I do about the subject that he was teaching. But that's a joke, of course. But we did know a lot more than what he was teaching the other kids, both in Hebrew and prayers.

INT: Did you witness or experience any antisemitism as a child?

ID: OK. That's one of the things that bothers me is I did not experience. But one guy, he happened to be a good friend, used to call me a bad name. And I would run after him and punch him. And he would run away and again, call me the same bad name. And I would go home. And I would say to myself, why am I hanging around with him? I had a lot of other friends, good friends, both in the religious group and around the neighborhood. We had a neighborhood full of kids. They were not big families. But there were houses with three stories, and every apartment had two kids, four kids, and we would gather, especially in our yard, was the gathering. And I remember now that I go to sleep, I can't sleep. I recall these things. There was a young girl. She was an early teenager. She went into arts and something, ballet and fine arts school for her four-year elementary and then two years to this school, until she was like 14 and 15. Then she didn't want to go to school anymore. But she was in the theater in school, so she would organize us kids and teach us. We would have plays. What plays? We charge $0.25 to neighbors to come and watch us. We were really a cohesive group. Sunday-- I don't mean Sunday. Summertime, we would gather in our yard, which had a little two retaining walls, everybody sitting there telling stories. Grown ups, kids would run all over and play hide and seek. I don't remember any antisemitism. [PAUSES] In school, I don't know if there was any. It didn't affect me. I wasn't exposed to it. Later on, we learned people above us, they was very friendly, but they joined the Nazis. And I don't know what happened. But [PAUSES] I knew there was antisemitism. Somebody in the sometimes in the store would make an antisemitic remark, and my mother would send him out of the store. I don't want to sell it to you, she would say. They would try to negotiate. And they would come to the lowest denominator, and then they would want more reduction in price. My mother couldn't give it to them, and they would mention something antisemitic. And my mother would say, I don't want to sell it to you. Get out of my store. So that was the strength of my mother's character, and shows that there wasn't that much of this antisemitic display. Now, later on we'll talk about it. Excuse me. [DRINKING] Go ahead, please.

INT: How aware was your family?

ID: How what?

INT: About-- how aware, how much did your family know about what Hitler was doing in Germany?

ID: My family knew everything. My father was coached in a cheder when he was a child. He used to tell a story about how he would have to get up early in the morning in the snow and go to the cheder. And we used to make fun of him. You know, he would say, snow up to here. And we would say boots up to here. You know all that? We didn't believe him, but he could conduct a service. He could do everything, except he was bashful when it comes to socializing. So he didn't get involved in synagogue functions. But he taught us, me and my older sister, everything he knew. My younger two sisters were protected from his teaching. He was a violent teacher. [PAUSES] He taught us with slaps. Boom, boom, if you didn't learn. So it behooved us to learn, the very old fashioned way.

INT: In September of 1939, Germany invaded Poland. How did that impact you and your family?

ID: Well, we could take it a step back, if you allow me to. This wasn't September that did it. It was October 1935 where they had a Kristallnacht. And we realized how bad this situation is with Hitler. And he took Poland. And he took first half of Czechoslovakia, and then the rest of Czechoslovakia. I said Poland. I'm sorry. He took Austria first, then he took Czechoslovakia in little segments. And then, because he needed, I guess, to go toward Russia, toward the oil fields and get some of the oil to run his machinery, war machinery, he attacked Poland. And then that was officially declared the start of Second World War. In our mind, it started with Hitler was appointed chancellor or head of the German government, and he started persecuting the Jews. And in our opinion, Hitler had two objectives. One was to conquer the whole of Europe and the rest of the world with his allies. And second was to destroy the Jewish communities, destroy them, kill them, wipe them off the map, all of the Jews. And every step he took was a step toward his achievement of his goal. And I might say, which I'll say later, but he almost achieved his goal in both ends. [PAUSES] And Poland was just the next step after Czechoslovakia.

INT: Had any refugees from Germany or Austria or Czechoslovakia, anywhere, had any refugees come through your area running away from the Nazis?

ID: Bless your soul. I'm glad you asked that, because I keep forgetting it. The few Jews that could escape came down to our area. That was before Yugoslavia was attacked and later came.

Well, they couldn't come. They wanted to go to United States, or England, or even France. But countries sealed their border. Everything was sealed. And so a few Jews that could escape, they came from Poland. I would see in our shul lots of new faces, lots of old Orthodox attire with beards, which we didn't see before. And they would have their Eastern wall position They go to the same place every praying session. And they would come to Split. Split had a Jewish community of roughly 200, 210 souls. But we were pretty well organized and active. We had a Zionist club, a regular social club, and Jewish community, and we spend, me and my sister, we went to the Jewish club, to the Zionist club every Saturday and Sunday. Saturday, we would go there, sneak before going to the service upstairs. And after the service, we would go there. Our mother would let us [PAUSES] organize games. Sunday, we would come to this Zionist club. I remember from the time I was third grade, I would come to the Zionist club. They would assign us to the group, groups, and they would have teaching sessions. And then we would have some productive sessions. Then we would go out, maybe go to the city, walk along the waterfront. Maybe we rent horses and ride them in the country. Maybe we go rowing the boats, do something. It was well organized with a leader of what-- 20, 22, 18, I guess that age. So as far as social life, we had plenty of organized activities in Split, and it was strictly Jewish. We had, like I said, social activities around our neighborhood. But Jewish was more pleasant. And especially around holidays, we go to the service, come out of the service, and go to the club. We call it [? can. ?] I think it means nest or something in Hebrew.

We would learn a few words in Hebrew, dances, sing songs. And I knew "Hatikvah" when I was four years old.

INT: I had asked you about refugees coming in. What did these refugees tell you?

ID: What did what?

INT: The refugees that fled the Nazis, you said some of them came to your community. What kinds of things did they say?

ID: They were first to show to the German soldiers, and see, Germans had groups that followed their troops, the special groups that would go out and harvest the Jews in the new community that the army conquers. So they had to know where the Jews were. And there were these locals that were happy to point where the Jews were. And our friends, I don't know if they carried it that far, the ones living upstairs from us. But they got big positions in the Croatian government. Croatian was called independent Croatian Republic. That was organized by Germany. [PAUSES] Everything is in Serbo-Croatian, or should I say in Croatian? But Germans organized independent states. And these states march in the step that Germany requested them to. So this one guy from upstairs, [PAUSES] he got married right before the war. OK. But he became a big wheel in the German government. When Italians were there, he was head of the fish distribution in Split. And fish was a big item of trade. We had special markets for fish. People would go out in the bay, and fish, and come back. I'm talking there was an industry of fishing in Split. And in those days, I could distinguish one fish from another. But today, I may not. Anyway, so this guy became the head of Split's distribution. And we knew that they were Nazis. They put on Nazi uniforms and all that.

INT: How did your life change in April of 1941?

ID: OK. Like I said, Hitler was gobbling state by state, one after another. And after they attacked Poland and split Poland, between them and Russia, they turned around because they couldn't fight on two fronts. So they turned around and attacked France. France was, what should I say? Their soldiers could march in a line and they could ride horses and sabers, but they were not trained for the modern warfare. Germany, when Hitler took over, he gave them jobs in war-producing items, factories. And they were working like crazy, Germans. And everything was on bearings, roller bearings, and what else?

You know, the ball bearings, roller bearing, tanks, and no more horse driven cannons. They had everything streamlined, mechanized. Anyway, so France had decided to build a big line, the fence line between them and Germany. It was called Maginot Line, with all kinds of tunnels, everything. But Germans were a little bit smarter than French. They went around. Belgium, Netherlands and Luxembourg were on the other side of France, so they just marched into Belgium. British and French tried fighting them. In a couple of weeks, the fight was over because France was not ready to fight a mechanized war. So then Germans moved into France. And they took everything. You know, we knew that they were marching. Who's next? We knew we were going to be in line. Our country was run by a young king. He hadn't taken over yet the throne. His uncle was in charge, and he signed the German-Italian pact. It was called Tripartite Pact that Germany had all these countries sign. What that meant, Germans can harvest all the raw material, steel-- that's not steel, it's iron. It's converted to steel. So they would steal the iron and take it to Germany. They would steal bauxite from Yugoslavia, take it to their country and convert it into aluminum and steel. So we knew what they were doing. And Yugoslavia had signed up with Germany. Then at one point, our king decided that he's 18 years old. He took over the government and he broke off from Germany. We knew what was going to happen. I knew that were-- Germans were going to attack our country. I knew that the minute there was festivities in the country that we are free from the Germans. I knew next step was German militarized units would come in. And they did. April 6, 1941, I was awakened by a sound of explosions, one, two. So what is that? Nearby, we had-- what they call? They were digging up for bauxite, for the aluminum. But also, they were digging up for cement something. But that couldn't be the sound, because I was used to that sound. Anyway, we went outside. I looked up and there's planes, and there's an explosion right behind planes. So I knew there was a bombing. And, you know, it was commotion. And we knew Germans and Italians were going to march in. And we just stayed suspended there for a while. We didn't know what was going to happen. But we, the Jews, we had a pretty good idea. Like I said, the Germans had taken Hitler's word, conquered countries, and killed the Jews. At the time they marched into Yugoslavia, they had taken about eight or so countries, and there was nothing we could do. They had signed pacts-- they call it a pact, with other European countries. And these other European countries were providing soldiers for the Russian front and all that. But I'm getting ahead of myself. But we knew they were going to come and, OK, nothing happened for three, four days. Then we started seeing Italian soldiers in groups of three or five marching through the city. That was like the military guards, you know? There was I guess patrols. And then we started seeing posters on the walls throughout the city every day, a new poster. First poster says this is Dalmatia has been taken over by Italians. And we expect the population to follow the instructions. First instruction, you have to turn in all your weapons. Who does not turn the weapons will suffer serious consequences. Next thing was about Jews. Jews have to report to some place where they will be registered. That gave us the idea that registration and Jews come. You know, we knew about what they had done to some communities. We didn't know about Kyiv and all that yet. But local population would help the Germans locate the Jews. If you don't report and they kill you on the spot. So we said to ourselves-- to ourselves, the Jewish community was advised by our, quote, "leadership," we'd better report. Let's see what happens. So everybody reported, signed in, and they were told you will get the ID cards. Everybody had to have ID cards. And the rest of the population had to get ID cards. But the police will issue the cards to us. And they would stamp the cards. not with the little words, but letters. Cards were like this big. You open, and first letter here, second, third, fourth, fifth, so you could not hide anything. So when somebody asked for your ID, immediately they know you're Jewish. Well, all right, they register we live where we live. And they told us not to move unless we get their approval. To us, that didn't mean much. People were moving around and all that. So we were really surprised to see Italians behaving the way they did. Because in Italy, they had-- I don't know whether to say, imprisoned or harvested Jews from all areas. And they showed their antisemitism in Italy. And they took Jews to concentration camps. And concentration camp is a misleading word. It is concentration, but it's forced labor. So if they did that in Italy, took Jews into forced labor, how bad is it going to be for us? So we were a little bit afraid. And life continued.

We were told to register. We were told to follow the curfew. We were issued food stamps. I call them food stamps, because everybody knows food stamps, a card with stamps marked And you had for bread, you had 31 little coupons that the baker had to cut and paste on a piece of paper. Otherwise, he doesn't get any flour. So we had bread ration. We had pasta ration. We had dry cereal ration, like beans and whatever, soybeans, and you know, little things that-- rice, little things that you could cook and eat. That was all rationed. And the rations were very small. And I want to put one anecdote. When my sister and I were coming home, we saw a line forming in a grocery store near our house. And we said, let's go see what they're selling. We got in the line, what are they selling? Potatoes. See, that's how scarce everything was. Even potatoes that were not rationed at first, but later they were rationed. So if my sister sends me home to get coupons, so we can buy potatoes, and it was limited quantities. So line moved to the head. The boss says, let me see your ration card. Oh, he says, you're Jewish. I'm sorry I can't sell you until the whole line gets their food. Jews come the last That was our destiny, I guess. My sister cried, but I didn't. OK. But next commotion was the Italians told us we are not allowed to go to any places where people gathered. That meant movies, parks, and recreation, [PAUSES] on the beach, wherever. We couldn't even come to the city square or main avenues. We had to walk on a narrow street. We, the kids, were informed by individual letter, we're sorry, we cannot accept you in the school. I registered for six grades. They send a letter, very polite letter. You know, dear Mr. Danon and family, we're sorry, but Isak is not allowed to attend school because you are Jewish, simply that. We regret the inconvenience kind of thing. Anyway, so we were not allowed to go to school. And all the teachers lost their jobs. All the state and local employees lost their job. There was immediate dismissals-- hospital employees, that meant doctors, nurses, what do you call-- orderlies, everything, police, firemen. And then even the employees of larger firms in Split, because it was a center. They had a lot of like insurance, and banking, and all that, all banks had to dismiss their Jewish supervisors. But first, they were instructed to teach their subordinates how to do their job, and then they're fired. You know, this was a comical thing. And you couldn't have a maid.

A Jewish maid you could. But Jews were not allowed to employ house assistants. You know? Or in the store we had an assistant, but she needed that job. So it was a charitable employment, and nobody said anything. But anyway, life for the Jews was restricted. And another incident, my cousin was a friend of mine. I mean, we played around together, so he invited me to lunch one Saturday after synagogue. So OK, we were-- I went to his store.

