8:00. Anyway, so he says, if we ask his father if Isak can come to lunch. Yeah, of course. And then we were going to their house. And he says, we passed near a bakery, a very nice bakery that sold sweet stuff, you know, pastry, all kinds of good stuff. [? Yacky. ?] And he says, let's get something for after lunch. So we went there. Everybody, my cousin and I, we chose what we want for ourselves, the gooey stuff. And he chose for the family and we went to pay. And then we noticed a sign in the window. It said, Jews are not allowed here. Jews, you turn our stomach. Jews, you pollute the air. And you know something, all kinds of things. And we said to the guy-- to the girl, oh, you don't want us. OK, keep the stuff. She says no, no, no, no. They came. They put these signs in the window. I had nothing to do. They told me to keep it there. You just select what you want. Come here. Pay me and go home and enjoy it and come back. So we had both sides. You know, sometimes the food was not available for us locally, and sometimes the guys-- I mean, the guys would even take the German special troopers to our house, and sometimes they would be nice and polite to us. But we were still dealing with Italians at that point. [PAUSES] But there are guards going--

INT: Continue.

ID: OK. Now, Italian guards would sometimes stop in. Did she give you stamps? Sometimes people didn't have stamps. In the country, people would go there, their stuff that they buy, it doesn't last forever. The stamps are just for one thing that if they buy it, it gets torn up. So what-- they would come and they would say, I want such and such material for a suit, but I don't have any stamps. Excuse me. [DRINKING] But my father--

INT: I do want to ask--

ID: Yeah. My father would say, OK, you bring them to me next time. So the Italians would stop people with the wrapping. Where did you buy this? They go into our store. You come with me to the store. You know, this is the three, group of three or five. One of them would wear black shirt. That means fascists. They were the worst kind, associated with Germans. Anyway, where did you buy this? Show me. Show me. Come on to the store. Where are the coupons for her? My father said, I dropped all the coupons in the bag. Show me her numbers. He didn't have it. OK, close up the store. And I'm going to have somebody come take all your stuff from you and all that, terrorization. And then what they were really after was a bribe. I'm not talking about that. Because that was better than the Germans did. But if we had a friend in there one time that suggested to the guy, he says, why don't you buy some stuff. If you don't have money now, you pay them after the war, you know? And they took that. So my father gave them-- I know one guy, three yards of a fabric that was worth a fortune because it was manufactured in pre-war England. And they say, I need matching shirts, you know? They were bold enough to ask for more. But anyway, so that's how they interfered with our lives. But so far, we didn't get hit.

INT: I was just going to ask you. Was there violence or destruction of Jewish property?

ID: What?

INT: Was there violence or destruction of Jewish property?

ID: Oh, no. We-- I will tell you the story. I happened to be 13 years old at that time. I mean, I lived under Italians from when I was 11 to when I was 13. And I'm a Jewish boy. And I love this Jewish home. I have to have a bar mitzvah. Bar mitzvah is a celebration that all Jewish boys go through. [PAUSES] You want to make a comment? Oh. All Jewish boys are called upon to come to the synagogue, whether they are very observant or a little less so, come to the synagogue, read special verses from the Torah. Torah is our scroll that maintained the five books of Moses, read from there, read a little bit about the prophets, and then give a speech. Well, me, I'm a smart Jewish boy. I read from the Torah and I chanted. I did it. I still remember my part. I did the other part about the prophets and all that. That part I don't remember too well. And then my speech, I said to everybody, up to now I've been a little squirt. I've been playing around. But now I'm serious. I'm a man. I'm 13 years old. I'm a grown up. I take my grown-up responsibilities seriously. So in honor of my getting to be a grown up, I make a commitment to come to the service, religious service, every week at least one time. And most likely, it will be either Friday night, or Saturday morning, or both. See, we start the weekend Friday night going to Saturday, up to Saturday evening. Saturday evening is Sunday already. In the Bible, it says in Hebrew, and there was evening and there was day, first day. And there was evening, and there was day, second day. So that's how we say. They start with evening when the sun goes down, and goes through the night into next day. OK. So the very next weekend, I got dressed real nice. And I said to my younger sister who was home, I'm going to service. And I made a commitment. Let me go tonight. And she says, I'd like to come with you. I said, come on. So we went. And my father's store was not too far from the synagogue. Actually, the synagogue was at the northern end of the city, main square, north-- northern end. And my father's store was south about a block. And so my sister and I said, why don't we go to our store and see if our father can close the store and come with us to the service. That seemed like a good idea. So we got to the store and we asked our father if he can close the store and come with us. He says, wonderful. We have just this one customer. And he tries begging the customer to come Monday. I'll sell you everything you want. I really have good stuff, but Monday. And that customer or client insisted on having it Friday night because she would take it to the tailor over the weekend and all that. So we were delayed. And then after the customer left, we closed up. We had to bring the stuff in. My father used to display outside, so it goes back in the store. He locks it up. We had a heavy wooden door with a lock that I couldn't lift at my age. Anyway, so we rushed to the synagogue through the city square, and we were making an infraction because we were not allowed to the city square. But city square had outdoors chairs in one side with a restaurant and an outdoor restaurant on the other side, with music and dancing. That was the norm. But here, we look. Nobody outside. Gee, wonder what happened. And then we hear screaming and yelling from the side where the synagogue is. They're coming out and soldiers running after them, hitting them with the butts of their rifles, kicking them, knocking them on the floor, kicking them again, punching them. And my father recognized some of the people that were getting hit. And we were in the-- he was in the panic. And he says, they're attacking the synagogue. I know. And then he took me and my sister by the hand and went the opposite direction. And we went all around the city. We didn't want to meet anybody or anything, all around the city, and we went to her house. And we told our mother. And she was afraid. She had prepared Friday night dinner like she did every Friday. And she was afraid what's going to happen to us, not only us, but everybody else in the community. Anyway, near us, one of the younger rabbis, there were two rabbis, young guys, one was single and he had rented a room in the vicinity of our house, or our apartment. So we went to his apartment, to see if he's home. We wanted to find out what's happened-- what happened. Anyway, we came there. And the landlady says, sorry, he's not home yet. So we waited outside for about 20 minutes or so. And we see him coming across the field, the same field that we came across to our house. And he's limping and he has a bloody hankie on one eye. And oh, rabbi, what happened? And he told us what happened. Just as the service was starting, a unit of these Italian-- he never knew whether it was soldiers or the fascists in military clothes, but they had the arms. They had bayonets. And they had rifles. And they came, took the rifles and said, OK, everybody out. They told the rabbi, tell everybody out. He says, we just started the service. We don't care. Everybody out. So everybody was told, rabbi says, I'm sorry. The military wants us out. So all the people from the shul started coming out. And there were these steps, marble steps, leading from the synagogue down to the cul-de-sac, which leads to the square. And people as they were going to this marble step, the Italian fascists had created-- what would you call-- on both sides, they positioned themselves. As the people were going down the steps, they would clobber them with butts of their rifles, and punch them, kick them. And they did a lot of damage to people. And they didn't care if you were a man or a woman. And as the Jewish people, the congregants were coming out to the square, you heard yelling and screaming. And we were close to it. So my father takes me and my sister, well, I said that part. Anyway, so now we knew that there was a problem. And we went to sleep. My mother was crying. I don't know what my father or my sister said. My older sister had left home and gone to the mountains to join the partisans. I'll give you an idea of what partisans were. There were local people who did not look kindly to either Italian or German occupation. And they escaped the life in the city and went to the mountains where they formed units. If they got weapons, they stole them from Croatians, and from Italians, and from other Serb renegades. And anyway, there were a lot of those. And they created units. My sister went with her friends. She didn't say good night. She didn't say goodbye. One night, she disappeared. So that was about my sister. And--

INT: Excuse me. When did she do that?

ID: She went, I guess, a couple of weeks before we went.

INT: What year are we talking? What month? Can you give me a time frame?

ID: We did-- I was 13 years old. I didn't know. She was somebody that would confide in me. But she didn't confide in that because my parents would not let her go to the mountains and take up the fight, you know? And she was 15 years old, a little squirt. We were all small structure. I'm 5 feet tall, 5 and 1 inch. At my highest, I was 5' 5". My son is a little over 6 feet, but we were all small.

INT: When did your sister join the partisans?

ID: I'm sorry.

INT: When did your sister join the partisans? Was that in '42, when you were 13?

ID: No.

We were not influenced by her. Their word around us is we're such and such young man, he's up there. He went up. We knew what everybody meant. The ladies would come to our store to buy some material. And they would ask, you know, if you can reduce the price. What is it for? My son, he went up. He went up to the mountains. We knew what that meant. The local population was pretty much opposed to Germans. But for every good side of the coin, there was the bad side. Anyway, [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] we knew that something is wrong after this. oh, yeah. No, I didn't finish. Next morning, my sister comes to my room bright and early and says, "Can we go to the city and see if anything happened?" Yes, we went. First, we walked near a photo shop. There wasn't a single camera in that store. The door was ajar, wide open. We walked to the synagogue. There were two Carabinieris marching up and down around the yellow tape. This was on the plaza, yellow tape. We approached the tape. There were remnants of a big bonfire. And in there, there were books, prayer books. There was Torah scrolls, half burned. There were all our holy objects. They were strewn all over the place.

You could see that somebody was trying to break everything. And they couldn't something, well, it was thrown in the fire. You know, the whole community, the books were in the synagogue. Now it was burnt. Even the drapes were in the fire half burned. The chair with the rabbi and the dignitaries sit were on fire. And then we went home. But we said, let's see other Jewish stores. We went to our uncle who had a cosmetics store. They had broken the display window. They entered through the display. Everything was broken. What wasn't stolen was broken, even the shelving. We went to another store, Jewish store, all broken up. And then we went to our store. As I mentioned before, our store was a hole in the wall, heavy wooden doors. You can see dents where they tried to hit with a heavy object. They couldn't break the locks or chain. I need to take a break.

INT: OK. We'll take a break now. Can we-- OK, we are back now. And you were telling me about a lot of destruction that was going on, but I wanted to ask you again, when did your sister join the partisans? What year are we again?

ID: My sister went to the partisans to the mountains, maybe two weeks before this took place, before this attack on the-- on our synagogue.

INT: And when was the attack on the synagogue?

ID: It was in-- let me get organized.

In September '41, I guess. No, I'm sorry. I'm sorry. It was one week after my bar mitzvah, which would be in June.

INT: What year?

ID: 1943. [PAUSES 7 SECONDS] Let me see.

INT: And had any Jews been deported from your area?

ID: Did any what?

INT: Had any Jews been deported, sent away from your area?

ID: I'm not hearing. Have any--

INT: Had any Jews been deported or sent away from your area? Were any Jews sent to concentration camps?

ID: Oh, yes, yes, yes. OK. The other Jews had escaped, I say a lot in numerically a lot, but proportionately very little number, very small number. Who was able to escape, escaped, came to Split or to the area. And after a while, there were quite a few, and they were very visible. Like if there were Germans, there would be talking loudly on the beach in German with a lot of make up. And we knew they were Jewish by their behavior. And I guess Italian authorities knew that. And also, by this required registration. So if they follow the registration with Italians like they were forced to do, then Italians knew exactly where they were, where they live, and they would come and get them, and they would intern them. There were a couple of islands. One was called R-O-B, Rob. And one was called Krk, K-R-K. And they would intern them over there. And that would be like, [PAUSES] well, wouldn't be forced labor, but they would be forced to maintaining themselves. They would make them work to sustain themselves. In other words, two-- they would accomplish-- the Italians with accomplish two objectives. One was to satisfy the Germans that they were picking up all the Jews and putting them in concentration camp. And the second one was in this concentration camp, they didn't want the Jews to be a burden on them. So they would put them to work to produce goods for themselves. So Italians were not nice to these people.

They would-- Jewish immigrants would disappear from one day to the next, whether they would escape from Split or whether the Italians would take them. But on these islands, there was concentration, like a concentration camp.

INT: What did you know about how Jews were being treated elsewhere by the Nazis? Did you know about Auschwitz or some of the other camps?

ID: Did we know about what happened to them or--

INT: Yeah, during the war, at this time period, 1943, how much did you know about what was happening to Jews in other places?

ID: I knew everything. When I say I knew everything, since I was six years old, we knew what Hitler was doing in Germany, and we knew what he was doing in Poland and other countries where there were a lot of Jews. But I mean, just one event like in Kyiv where there was a ravine called Babi Yar. And they-- as soon as Germans took over the city, the local population helped them out. And they got all the Jews in this ravine. And they had machine gun nests all around, and they machine gunned the Jews. And they say that 150,000 were killed. That's what we knew as kids. I knew when I was six years old what they did at Kristallnacht. And I knew what they did in Poland. And I knew what they did in every other country. And when I say we were next, like Yugoslavia, we were trembling because we knew what had happened, and especially in Russia and Poland.

Russia, I mean, there were a lot of Jews in Russia. That means Ukraine. That means Southern Russia. That means Belarus and all that. And in Poland, there was a lot of Jews in a different area. I don't need to say Ukraine, because that was known as Russia in those days. But we knew the Germans were-- what the Germans were doing. And even in France, in occupied France, and quote, "non-occupied," which Germans controlled, same thing was happening. And other countries, what countries are they--

INT: I'm concerned about your experience. I want to know your experience. I don't care about the other countries now.

ID: I knew all about that.

INT: Did you and your family have any idea about maybe escaping, or maybe joining the partisans, or doing something?

ID: Me and my family, I was a little squirt of 13 years. And my family-- and my mother would cry when she would hear about things happening. My father would just talk with his friends about what the Germans are doing and what they're doing in other countries without Germans, about Bulgaria-- and-- what's the country that Germans took? They didn't took-- oh yeah, Denmark. They didn't take Spain. But the guy in charge, I forget his name, they were all German collaborators. And I knew all that. And I knew what was happening to the people in Poland because there was a concentration of Jews there, and Germans were just trying to destroy them, the easier way they could, until final solution. That was the word that was going around. And in this Jewish Zionist club, we-- I could say we studied about that. But we were told, we were informed we-- excuse me. [COUGHING]

INT: Gesundheit.

ID: Yeah, Jewish youth at that time was very well informed of what was going on in the world, and unfortunately, nothing happy. When my sister went, I was ready to go myself. But what can I do? But we were still thinking that maybe something might happen to us if the Germans walk in and Italians have to show that they also Jews-- haters of Jews. So my father got two backpacks.

And one was smaller than the other. The smaller one was for me. Am I getting out further from the subject here?

INT: No, go ahead.

ID: OK, and we packed those backpacks. And the idea was that if Germans walk in, the first thing they do is pick up all the Jewish men. Me, being 13, I'm a man. So they'll pick me up too for hard labor. And these people that went-- they were picked up for hard labor, I don't want to use concentration camp, because that's too light. Concentration was someplace where you go to spend time. But anyway, [PAUSES] we-- excuse me. The local population in Split was disappearing, the young people. And where are they? They went up. That meant they went up to the mountains. And that's what we knew. In the store, people would come, buy material. They want to make something to take up to their son. These were volunteers, secret, they would disappear at night. You never knew where the Italians picked them for their torture camps, and which they had. My uncle was taken-- that pretty personal-- [PAUSES] or whether they went to the mountains. So people were disappearing. And that's what my father said to me. If they come, let's be prepared. And we'll go to the mountains, you and I. And Mom and two younger girls were going to stay back, see what life gives. If it gets any tougher, they're supposed to go to the milk lady, to her little village and hide there. And that's what did happen. We-- I'll tell you how we went up to the mountains. But my mother was left, and she went to the milk lady. Milk lady kept her for a month or so. And then she told her, "I'm afraid. The Germans are all over." And she had to send her away. And my sister wrote a book about her travels from one relative of the milk lady to another, to a little-- to a bunch of villages, and ended up in the partisan, what they call [? zbyeg. ?] [? Zbyeg ?] is the Croatian word for those who have escaped. A group that escapes altogether that's known in Croatian as [? zbyeg. ?] So people were leaving their homes and running to the mountains, men, women and children. In our case, my father and I, we packed these backpacks. I know in mine, I put an extra pair of shoes, another sweater, a couple of shirts, some socks, underwear, and some Zwieback. Zwieback, for those who don't know, it's a very hard like a pretzel, but hard. And you can chew on that any time you want to. You don't need heating or anything, if you have good teeth. And it will sustain you. That's what they give to the soldiers when they go to the front lines because they can't cook. So anyway, that's what I had. And [PAUSES] eventually, it happened. Shall I tell that story?

INT: Are you going to tell us how you got to the partisans?

ID: Yeah.

INT: Please.

ID: OK. One night, not much later, there was one night between Saturday and Sunday, there was knocking at the door. "Mr. Danon, Mr. Danon, the Germans are coming!"

Oh, I skipped a very important point. Americans and British had invaded Italy, British on the side of the Adriatic, Americans on the side of Mediterranean. And they were advancing. And Italians went up. They surrendered. [COUGHING] And these Italians that surrendered, they-- their soldiers, they dropped the weapons and they went by any means available back to their homes in Italy. I was going to say camels and backpack. I don't know how they went home, but they crowded the railroad. [COUGHING] And the Italian government collapsed. So Americans and British had taken over control of Italy, but then Germans didn't like that. So they took a bigger part of Italy that Americans and British did not occupy. Anyway, when the Italian soldiers threw up their hands, they also left a lot of weapons. So the local population grabbed those weapons, and they were requested by the partisans to join the partisan troops, join them, come with us up to the mountains. And many went up the mountains. And we would have city gathering at the city square. And there would be one of the partisans saying, "Here you were under occupation. Now you're free. You are free, but the rest of the country is not free. Come on to the mountains." [COUGHING] The partisans had all these new people and new arms that were coming from Italians. They were better organized. And they were in a position to attack the German units and Croatians. Not even talking about Croatian, a Croatian, meaning the ones that joined the Germans-- there were a lot of those. I call them traitors. But they call themselves independent. Anyway, so lot of people, [PAUSES] when after these people were banging on the door, "The Germans are coming, the Germans are coming! Mr. Danon!" They were not talking about me as Mr. Danon. Talking about my father. And the least likely people did that. One Italian lady did it, and one that was Croatian. Her family was in German units fighting on the Russian front. But anyway, they warned us. And we grabbed our backpack, went through the back door, and went through the fields. And our house was-- see Split was located along the coastline, spread out really big spreading all over coast, a lot of beautiful beaches. But anyway, if you go 10 miles inland, you would encounter big, heavy mountains. They're called Dinaric mountain chain. And we didn't have any names for them except part of the chain. Anyway, and that's where partisans were active. And people from the local area were leaving.

We went at maybe

3:30, 4 o'clock, when my father woke me up and said, "Say goodbye to Mom. And we're going." He and my mother subdivided what money they had. And we went up. And that's what going up meant. We went to the mountains looking for partisan units. Now, partisans were in the city, and they were defending the city. At one point, they had a lot of local people that took Italian arms and were fighting with the German and Croatians. But you cannot fight and organize units with what you got, a rifle here and a rifle there. So the Germans and the Croatian collaborators, they broke through the partisan lines. [WIPING NOSE] And they were driving through Split. My sister, Sara, she gave this story on this by reference. And she has pictures of Split where it says, "death to the fascists, freedom to the people," in big bold letters on their wall. And German motorized not one unit, but tanks and other heavy weapons by marching in town and blocking the sign a little bit. So it shows partisans had done a lot of campaigning. And young people had left their home and gone up to the mountains. I was too young. And my father was, well, I would say old, until I came to the partisans. And then I saw that there are even older people there. And you didn't ask the religion. They all went. All of the Serbians went, and well, there was a lot of communists in the Yugoslav nation. And they were suppressed by the government. But then when they got a chance, they sprouted up. And they started encouraging people to join the People's Liberation Army. That was the official name, People's Liberation Army. So my father and I walked and walked. And we were looking for the People's Liberation Army. We met some other people looking for them. And somebody told us, a lady recognized my father from his store and says maybe her husband knows. And her husband had a brother in another village who was a partisan, and somebody whom he knew, knew where the partisans were active. So they took us there. What is a partisan? It's a local person who is opposed to the rule that's imposed on them, and hopefully has a weapon and a uniform. When I came, the only uniforms they had was like a beret type military with a red star in front. So that was the partisan uniform. Later on, when Americans and British started shipping and dropping supplies and other equipment, we had British uniforms with that kind of a cap. But anyway, we met a lot of people going up to the mountains. And we joined a group that were in one of the villages going from one place to the other. We joined a group of partisans. Like I said, they were dressed in their own clothes with a beret. I don't know who was making the berets. But anyway, eventually, I got one too. So we were in this group. They told us to hide. Lots of trees-- lots of trees are lower end, but nothing but rocks up above. And that's what we had to do. Hide in the daytime, be active at night. That first night, they went out. They said, let the kid sleep. That was me. Let the kid sleep. I heard them say that. Anyway, so they went. And what did they do? Come back next morning, I find out they got bags, a couple of bags of dry beans, pasta, and some other kind of beans. They attacked a farm of a family that had joined the opposition. They were with the Germans. They were with the Croatians quislings, [COUGHING] the country called independent Croatian Republic established by Germany. So these guys come back with that. When I woke up in the morning and my father told me. I says, so for that, you guys go out there and fight with the guns and all? He says, yeah, everybody's got to eat. So we had food to eat.

Sometimes they had a mule. Yeah, I don't know where they got it. They requisitioned it from a peasant. And that carries either weaponry or food supply. So they had some of those, but they were not cooperating. When a mule, you said go forward, she would firm up her feet and go backwards. Anyway, so that was the first night they came with food supply. The next-- the next day, they say we have to hide. So my father is called and he comes back. He says, they want you there too. So they decided-- oh, there was another 12-year-old kid with us. He had escaped from his family, because he was opposed to German occupation. So they decided that if this other kid knows how to go with them, I should go too. So next evening, we went. My job and this kid, was be a courier, runner. They didn't have telephones from one end to the other. And they wanted to surprise the enemy, enemy meaning German soldiers or Croatian, or later, even renegade Serbs which Germany had armed and commanded. Anyway, so they were going out. And they needed somebody to go from one location to the other and tell them how you fixed, you have enough weapons, who is where, why don't you spread out a little bit more, that kind of order. And they would put it on a piece of paper and sign it. And we have to run to the position. And that's what we did, this kid and I. And then-- may this-- [BACKGROUND NOISE]

INT: Can I ask you some questions--

ID: Please.

INT: --about the partisan group?

ID: Yeah.

INT: And then you can come back and tell me more in a minute. But how large was the group? How many people were there do you think?

ID: Every locality had its own partisans. One group didn't know about another. And what they did, there was a command someplace hidden, because Germans had dropped paratroopers on the command a few times. And commander, a guy by the name of Josip Broz, he named himself nom de guerre-- that war names-- was Tito. And he was in charge of the People's Revolution uprising. And you would hear on the phone, not on the phone, on the radio about different accomplishments. And they would issue little pieces of paper the command of First Dalmatian Brigade. What is First Dalmatian Brigade? Well, it took place in Dalmatia. Must be first. It must be Dalmatian. It's first because they were the first bunch that organized themselves in that particular area. We had the [? Spalatino ?] from Split. We had a name, our own units. Whoever named themselves whatever.

INT: In your group where you were, how many people were there?

ID: About 10, 12 to start with. And you know, you have an attack, and they chase you. They attack you, the Germans and Croatians, and you end up separated. And I got separated from one unit and I happened to go to another unit, and they gave me a job, you know, throwing hand grenades. And this other kid knew how to do it before he even joined the partisans, he escaped from home. And-- but that was the word. If you don't meet your group, you're going to run into another partisan group. Don't be shy. Just tell them what group you were with. And that's all. Tell them--

INT: So it was-- [PAUSES] it was easy to join these partisan groups?

ID: I joined them next at night, and we went, and they had me carrying dynamite at the railroad crossing. And they attacked a guard. There was only maybe three, four people guarding. They would exchange every two hours and all that. But we were outnumbered them. [COUGHING] So we attacked.

INT: Did you have any training?

ID: No, there was no training. The training I got was they said, have the other kids show you how to throw a hand grenade. One thing that I learned is you count up to three. If the hand grenade doesn't go that way, then your arm goes that way, the opposite direction. If you throw it at 1 and 2, the other guy there might be smart enough to grab it and throw it back at you, and then your whole body will be messed up. So they scared me enough. And the other kid says, that's nothing. We used to throw at the other unit. He got misplaced, ended up with our unit. One kid, and he was no taller than I. I was considered a pint. You know? One second. [DRINKING]

INT: While you're taking your drink, let me get my question organized. Can you describe what the living conditions were like in your partisan group. Where did you sleep? How did you get food and clothes and medicine? Can you tell us about that?

ID: OK. At first, we lived in this partisan area where Germans were attacking on two sides. And we were supposed to derail their trains and do some mischief. Well, we did, but the Germans could repair it. They knew how. And we would always wonder what good have we done.

But during the day, we would hide. I know my father, they made him, they called it [? econom. ?] He was charged with providing food for these people. We joined another group, so it was a little bit more of us. Whether that group was a functional unit or whether it was just stragglers from other group, I didn't ask. But my job was help carry ammunition, be a courier, and when push comes to shove, throw hand grenades at the places where they designate. We also had to cut the wire and get under the wire. Germans used to have dogs. Croatians didn't. So it was easy going under the wire, and either make enough room for the guy behind you, or go near the window with a hand grenade ready to be activated. And that's what we were doing, this kid and I. And I forgot his name. He escaped from his family and he was there. Anyway, life was leisurely hiding at night. We were scared. But we did our job, which was me and this other kid, throwing hand grenades, and tried to get back to the location where we started from. If the Germans or the Croatian had moved out of their compound and attacked us because of their superior numbers, then we would disperse and try to converge at another location. I had changed two locations and-- in less than a week, I guess, until I learned a little bit how to traverse the countryside. And my father would arrange for cooking in the village hot meals. Otherwise, they had whatever thing each person had in their knapsack, if they had one or something, [? GIs. ?] They carried something with them like I carried mine. So if we got lost, at least you know you have some of your stuff. And my father at one point, I know that he was buying food for the unit. Whatever money he had, he was using it to go bribe the village so they'll give him some food or cook some food. And life was OK for a kid, 13 year old. It was like being on a picnic, you know? But then later, it got harder when they started chasing us. And one guy had a sickness, dysentery, and we were petrified that he's going to transmit it to the rest of the group. But anyway, we would-- we would try to do a little damage, the grown-up, and when they would send me I worked on the railroad one time. Another time, we turned the track around. It was automatic. You just turn them and destroy the mechanism. Anyway, and my father, I saw he was struggling because they expected him to produce, to have meals for them, and he couldn't always do it. And they would accuse him of being a fifth column. Fifth column is somebody who sneaks into your unit and spies on you.

Like a spy, ordinary spy was considered fifth column from a story about a guy having four columns outside the city and one column secret to the inside. But anyway, that's a different story. But my father was sweating, because he had never worked before. He said he had some kind of sickness that inflates the arteries. I didn't believe him. But I saw him carrying bags, and he was ready for the cemetery after that. Anyway, we were going like that.

INT: Were there any women in the camp or any families?

ID: We had women, not in this unit. We had women in other units. The fact that my sister was in one unit, wherever it was, I would say she would be in the First Dalmatian Brigade. There's a word, first hitting the mission brigade. Yeah power. Yeah, they gave themselves fancy names because they would hit the Germans and then escape or disperse. Anyway, in this unit, we didn't have any women, but we had two kids. [PAUSES] Let's see what I was going to say. Oh, my father, like I said, was accused of different things. And he didn't like his assignment. Well, anyway, we looked for other units. And one time, there was a boat going from one island to the next one. And we heard that the next island was better armed, and the Germans couldn't get to it. So my father said, let's go to that island. Now, partisans were not organized units. They were groups of people trying to harass the opposition. So we were just that group of people. Today, we were 12. Tomorrow, we may be eight, the next day 14 or 15. You know, different people joined our units to harass the Germans. So we, my father and I, got on one of these wooden boats. They had the sails. But nobody wanted to put on their sails, because sails were visible. They had motors. So they would go from one to the other island. There are hundreds of islands in-- along the Dalmatian Coast. But actually, they all belong to Yugoslavia. Some are bigger islands, some are smaller. Maybe there is maybe 5, 6, maybe 10 big islands. And the rest are small. Some are not even populated. And partisans took advantage of that. And when-- things improved for them when the British and the American recognized the partisans as a fighting unit. They started sending supplies over. And they would send them from Italy, either from Italy or they would release these boats from their bigger boats, I don't know, in the Adriatic. But we would see Americans and British soldiers among the partisan commands. And partisans will have shows like for the-- not 4th of July. Yeah, they had one 4th of July, but for Yugoslav Independence Day. And there would be in the front row, a couple of Americans in uniform and couple of British, and they would ask them to say a few words in any language. And we would say we are allies. That was the partisans were allies of the Americans and the British. And at one point, they started shipping supplies.

You want me to go into that or not yet? Not yet.

INT: A little bit.

If you could share a little bit of that.

ID: OK. [PAUSES] The Americans, I don't mean American-- British who were on our-- on the Adriatic side, started shipping supplies, but they wouldn't dock because they were afraid of the Germans. What they would do is come close. And at night, you see flashing lights. I didn't know what it was. But I was told it was Morse code. And the partisans would pick that as a message. And then we would go row-boating to that unit and unload the stuff they're bringing. It would be like medical supplies, food, even uniforms, British uniforms. I don't know what else. You know, but-- [PAUSES] So we would row. And once where we-- well, we traveled through the partisans, and we ended up in one of these islands. And I want to describe the unit. There was a unit of maybe 35 people there, partisans, and they were under a command of one guy. He was working in the [WIPING NOSE] machine shop. He was commanding officer. And he commanded the kitchen. They had the house. I don't know, maybe the owners escaped. And my father was the [? econom ?] from the other unit. So he got the job here, [? econom ?] meaning supply sergeant. And he got the job there. And I was placed in the machine shop. These guys would go out. And sometimes they had to go from the boat to the coastline swimming, and their weapon would get soaked in the salt water. Our job was to desalt it, and we had not benzene, but turpentine. And this kid and I would disassemble the rifles, and wipe them with the turpentine cloth, try to get them back in shape. And grown-ups would reassemble it. Anyway, so we were working in that machine shop. They had two positions, gun positions, on the Island. So they had about six or eight people occupying that. They had people driving the truck. I don't know where to. But at mealtime, there were like 20 people would come, and maybe three or four would come, women with something to put the food in, cook food. And my father would dish it out to them. They would go. Either they were taking it quote, "location." Location meant nobody knew where they were. Either they were taking them to a location or they were feeding the soldiers on the machine gun position. They didn't want them leaving. When I say soldiers, I'm talking partisans. Now, partisans were the organized units of the Yugoslav-- first it was Yugoslav Communist Party. Then they called it People's Liberation Army.

So People's Liberation Army was intended to absorb everybody. And everybody was called upon to stand up, rebel, join the fighting units in the mountains. Now, eventually [PAUSES] these fighting units would stop fighting when the Germans would send a big force, and we would just go from one island to the other. But anyway, my father-- and now this is something for the Jewish people will understand. He wanted to say the prayers every morning. But he didn't want to do it in the house where there were all these soldiers, and their language was not so clean all the time, and what was going on. So he would go around the other side, outside, and the other side facing the wall, and he would recite the prayer. That's the whole morning prayer. And he didn't put the cap. He would put his hanky around the ears and top of his head. And his lips would be moving. And somebody saw him saying the prayers. And he would announce the words. And somebody reported him. They denounced him. He has something in his ears and he's communicating with the enemy and all that. And my father says, no, no. And they had a kangaroo court. And this is what I used to kid our father. Comrade Danon, we know everything. Just admit and we'll go easy on you. That was his kangaroo court with the partisans. No, I'm not a traitor. I'm not. I'm a partisan. I was saying my prayers. Ah, they didn't believe that. I knew what was going on because he would sometimes sit at home, put the hanky, and say a prayer. Anyway, so they accused him. They had this trial. And they said, OK, go home. And we're going to watch you. And he escaped. He went from that island, where he went, he went to another island, third island. But I learned that later. I was by myself. This kid and I were two kids, you know. We had food. The cook would prepare food for us. Somebody else took my father's place to get food. And life was going on. I didn't miss my father's. Before that, he was an individual in an army unit, and I was another individual in an army unit. Well, then I got a note.

It says, command, you know? Present yourself to the command unit on this particular island. This particular Island was like Gibraltar, like Malta, where British had reinforced it. And it's located among the German's unit, conquered unit, all Germans and Italians all over. But here's the British, Malta an island, fortified. Here was Gibraltar, where you go from Mediterranean into Atlantic. And British controlled both sides, wouldn't let German submarines through. I don't know if they succeeded or not. Anyway, so I was supposed to report there. So I went there. I reported. Nobody knew what was what. So I just hung around for a while. And then I met a relative. She was a cook at the partisan command. That was islands command. They were defending all these islands and controlling. So she told me, "Your father has gone to Italy, and he would like you to join him." And that's a big step. First of all, how do I get to Italy? And second step is why go to a foreign country when they need me here? They need every able-bodied person. So I went to my commanding officer. And he says, that looks pretty official.

I will release you. And then I was released. Now how do I get to Italy? I was hanging around, doing nothing. So when a boat went to pick up supplies, I would go sometimes, help unload from their PT boat into our little rowboats, two or three rowboats. So one time, I guess I went. And I must have fallen asleep on the bench over there after lowering some maybe ammunition, maybe food. I don't even know what they had in that particular delivery. So next, I remember somebody shaking me. We're going. The boat is moving. I hear strange language being spoken. What is this? Where am I? And then one of the British soldiers knew a little Italian. Italian was one of my languages. So this is a good time to say I speak seven languages. But we'll take it a little later. Anyway, so in Italian, he explains to me, we're going to Italy. I said I didn't want to go. He said, "Well, you got on the boat, you fell asleep. Maybe we didn't see you." So here I get to Italy. What are they going to do with me? Ship me back, and the next time they go with supplies? In the meantime, there's a DP, that's displaced person camp, not too far from them. And he says there was a-- the words [? zbyeg ?] are used. There was the Dalmatians [? zbyeg ?] came here on their way to Egypt to the tent city. Not to confuse you, but British were taking civilians to Egypt to the Sahara-- not the real Sahara, but to a hot place. The tent city, they called it El Shatt, not too far from Cairo. And they already had 50,000 people there. British were feeding them instead of Italy. But these people came. Many came from Yugoslavia and many were these Jewish immigrants, Jewish immigrants that had come to Split and other Dalmatian locations that Italians had interned on two islands, Rob and Kirk, and they had put them there like in [? asylum. ?] They couldn't go anywhere. They were like in prison. Anyway, when I learned that, you know, I didn't like it. But maybe my father is there. So we were walking. I met kids a couple of days later. I met kids. We were playing soccer on the street. I see three guys walking toward us. And I'm kicking the soccer ball. I looked. I said to another soccer player, "I think I know that guy in the middle." And then the guy in the middle came closer. I said, "Hey, Dad, what are you doing here in Italy?" So that's my, what shall I call it, my high point. So my father told me he was situated there. They have camps. They have Jews and non-Jews. So we are-- we're Jewish, but Jews were already assigned to certain villas. These were villas that were owned by millionaires up north, and they couldn't come down because Germans had occupied their area, and they couldn't escape from the Germans. So villas were empty. So British used them as their refuge camp for escapees, people like us, people that had escaped from Italian [PAUSES] places of Jewish assemblage. Anyway, there was one building where they had 40 Jews, and there was a guy in charge. They called him kapo. And he would say 40 Jews and one kapo. That's from a story, A ballad about 40 thieves, if you know the story. Every kid knows that story, Aladdin. Anyway-- so--

INT: Can I stop you for a minute and ask you a couple of questions?

ID: Yeah, go ahead.

INT: I just want to clarify a few things. When you were with the partisans, were the civilians in the area helpful or not?

ID: I would say at that point, civilians were helpful. But if the partisans are forced to abandon that town or village, that's a different story. That's where you see the true color of these people. First, I know, my father would go and beg for food. And they would say, well, I can share with you. I have a large family. I can give you a couple of pounds, you know, of dry macaroni or something. Everybody was hoarding food. That's your way to survive. And when we were in Split, we had one room where we put bags of dry food, whether it be pasta, or beans, or whatever, legumes, rice. But sometimes they would get worms, and you're eating a worm. Don't mention it. Anyway you have more questions.

INT: Yes. How long did you stay with the partisans?

ID: OK. I'm still with the partisans here. I stayed, I'm going to-- just from September 10 to maybe January 15 or 20.

INT: And the year.

ID: Oh, 1941 into 1942. You know, if we had the big clock, as they have in New York, that descends 1942, that would be for us. But we didn't have that. New Year's just came and went.

INT: While you and your father were with the partisans, what did you know about your mother and your sisters?

ID: Nothing. My father asked me a few times. "What do you think happened to your mom and sisters, you know?" I said, "Dad, I don't know. I can't think of that. I'm a young kid. This breaks my heart, what we did to leave them on their own." And my father says, "Leave them. Maybe they can save themselves." We saved ourselves. If we were as a group, who knows? We talked about that. [PAUSES 7 SECONDS] But that's while I'm in the partisans, like maybe four or five months.

INT: And you were telling me now you're with your father in Italy.

ID: Right.

INT: And what happened next.

ID: He took me out of the group that I-- that I had been placed, he took me out. It's like kidnapping almost, because British had accounted for every body. OK. He took me out and we went to another camp, right nearby. [PAUSES] Let's see. Oh, our group, here's this thing. Our group, were-- there was no room in that 40 thieves. There were no place. My aunt was there. And she says, can you accommodate yourself here? Fine? If not, next building has room. So they were leaving that 40 people that were Jewish. They're special. They're talking about going to Palestine. But the others were the peasants who had committed themselves to the partisan cause. And now their lives are in danger. And they had escaped. So the British were taking them to Africa, to this place called El Shatt, the tent city. Well, they put me there and my father and I eventually got out of there, and we escaped one night when they were getting ready to ship out. My father came to wake me, woke me up and says, "We're getting out of here." So he had a peasant with a carriage, you know, the local peasant, the kind of carriage that he used for wheat or grapes. They grew grapes there. Anyway, so they took us away from partisans, away from-- [PAUSES] these people that are escaping, away from that. And we went on a train. On the train, the train dropped us off in a county seat. It wasn't a very big city. It was called Lecce, L-E-C-C-E, Lecce. And there were a lot of kids there. We asked them in Italian, of course, "Where's the next train." "Ah, you just missed it, the one that goes to Bari." Bari was a concentration of Jewish immigrants and Split immigrants. People from Split were going to Bari, and so were Jewish immigrants. And we wanted to go to Bari because my uncle was in Bari. We knew that, and a few other people we knew. And we tried for the next five or six mornings to make the train, and we were always late 10, 15 minutes, the train had left the station. So we settled for Lecce. We were staying in Lecce. And my father got a job. And I'll tell you how. [PAUSES] And we lived on the economy. [PAUSES 13 SECONDS]

I and my father lived with Italian people. [PAUSES] We rented a room with a lady and, well, her not real daughter, but a girl she had taken to care for, very nice two people. And we met other Yugoslavian immigrants who had been in Split and escaped from Split also. There was a restaurant called Chin-Chin, and that's where all the immigrants would meet, Chin-Chin, [PAUSES] British wording for so long, I guess. And we would go there. We met people. We befriended a lot of people, and those people told us about a convoy-- a convoy? A group of 1,000 refugees that was going to be allowed to go to United States from Italy. What do you need to do? You have to sign up. So we signed up, my father and I. We said, "What about my mother and two sisters and three sisters." Well, there were stories, all kinds of savagery that the Nazis had conducted upon the Jewish people in partisans and other places. But we didn't know details. And anyway, we signed up and I guess 10,000 others signed up. But whoever was picking people, they picked people from the--

I guess from the camps in Italy where Jews were housed by the Italians, [PAUSES] a place called Ferramonti. I don't want to clutter you with all kinds of city names. Anyway, we got--

INT: I think we have to take a break about now. I think we need to take a break.

ID: OK.

INT: OK. We are back.

ID: OK. There was this word spread out, spread around that the United States government [COUGHING] has invited 1,000 refugees to come to the United States. See, the gates were closed. Nobody could go into United States without a visa, and they're not giving visas. So here's a chance for people to go to the United States. So we signed up. Upon signing, we were informed that we have to go back to our country of origin after the war.

We would have signed anything at that point. Anyway, my father and I got accepted. And we had some good friends, some from Sarajevo, some from Belgrade that were in that group.

They wouldn't let them. Why? I don't know. We're speculating the different reason because they needed to earn their living. And how they did it is by finding who has ornaments, gold and stuff, and selling it at a profit. Everybody had to make a living. Anyway, so they told us to prepare for United States. And we did. This was in late June and--

INT: What year?

ID: 1944. Here is Americans, and British, and French Canadian, and whoever. It's assembling a heavy army in Great Britain to invade Europe. And the invasion of Normandy took place right then. And we asked-- oh what happened? Somebody comes and tells us, tells my father and me that my mother and two sisters are in this other camp. Oh, glorious. So how do we get to them? They arranged for them to board the train and come. And we were reunited. But we've been accepted to go to United States. So I told, I mean, I was my father's spokesman. We are not going to the United States unless you can get my mother and two sisters on board. Well, they said, "No, we'll have to scratch them." Well, it turned out scratching was more complicated than getting them on. So the Americans accepted my mother and two sisters on this convoy. And we all went to Naples by army trucks. We are now in Bari. And crossing that Italian Peninsula, it's not a lot, maybe two, three hours. But it's-- the army truck is not too good. Anyway, we got to Naples. They were-- they were trying to create the convoy.

You wouldn't let a single ship go through because that would be an invitation to German U-boats, you know, the submarines. So they were assembling a convoy. And the convoy was-- when they did assemble, it was 29 ships, 29 ships and four cruisers. I say cruisers. I don't know one from another. Maybe it's a destroyer. Maybe it's whatever, but a Navy boat. And they were going to be the guards to our convoy. The ships sailed from Naples Bay. By the way, Naples Bay was covered with crates of food. Germans learned about the convoy, and they bombed Naples harbor only two nights earlier. We were saved. We got on the boat. And they gave us special quarters all the way down, everybody, 982 people. We all got individual hammocks assigned to us. And I was on the third floor, one hammock, two hammocks, three hammocks like this, but on top of the first hammock. So I was pretty high up. And people above me, fourth, they would squirm. And I would like to punch them. Anyway, we were in these hammocks when the ship was loaded with everybody, including a lot of wounded GIs going home. The convoys started, and we went zigzagging. First, I don't know about geography, how the people are familiar. But from Italy, you could take a direct route to Gibraltar and then go directly to the United States. Uh-uh. That's dangerous. So we went toward Africa. We hugged the African coastline. And then we went through Gibraltar, and hugged the coastline, the African coastline, a little bit more, quite a bit more to get out of the way of German submarines. [PAUSES] I don't know if submarines were in the Mediterranean. But they were surely in the Atlantic, in the Atlantic Ocean. So we got our instructions. If you hear a sound or lights go on flashing a certain way, everybody goes downstairs. They cover it. Lights are out. And these people that like to smoke, they were dying from I don't know what. They were not allowed to smoke. But anyway, we had several of these during the trip from-- [PAUSES] well, through the Atlantic.

It took us 15 days from April 7th-- I mean from July 17 into August 2 or 3, depending on how you figure, we started approaching New York Harbor. We had to stop several times because there were German submarines. So the destroyers would take off and cruise around us and see if they can detect any German activities. And two or three times they were. But we reached New York. And how to describe the glorious feeling of 1,000 people when they saw these skyscrapers and the cars going on-- what is it, Long Island super highway, zooming back and forth. Whoever saw cars like that? In Split, we had four cars for the city, and they were taxicabs, black mind you. These were pink, green, yellow, red.

Anyway, happy scene when we passed near the Statue of Liberty. I was afraid that the boat was going to tumble over. If you can imagine, 1,000 people rushes to the side and to see the statue. Well, it didn't tumble over. We came. And we stopped there. We got-- we went and docked. [PAUSES] And the ship went a little bit further. It didn't stop, didn't dock in New York Harbor. They docked in a place, and the name of the town escapes me. But I know Frank Sinatra was from that town. So if anybody remembers Frank Sinatra, the singer, that's where our boat went. And we docked there. And would you believe, after we docked, in 10 minutes, the military marching band comes and plays music. Oh, what a country, we say. Not only they invite you there, they feed you well through the trip. By the way, we did more vomiting than eating, but that's another story. And they feed you well, they treat you well, they entertain you, and now the music. Well, first disappointment, when they unloaded the wounded GIs, the music stopped playing, and the band turned around and marched away. They didn't come to play music for us. They came for the wounded heroes. So, OK. We couldn't unload until they marked us all up. And we were defined as unassigned baggage. We had tags on us, put on us different colors for different barracks, where we would stay. And then we were unloaded. Again we were de-loused on the docks. It took two or three days for that. And we went-- we were put on the Lackawanna railroad, in a sleeper Pullman. What a country-- they give you a sleeper to go to your next destination. We go at night. They wake us up in the morning. Wake up. Everybody out. Get your luggage and our carry-on luggage. The rest came some other way, by trucks, I guess. And we unload and the train leaves. When we unloaded, we saw a barbed wire fence. Oh, my God, from one fence to another. We said, enough of that. But they said in there, everybody in. And they closed the fence. They locked it. And we were there inside. And they did not let us go out. Some people had friends or relatives on the border that had heard that we were coming, and they knew where we were. We didn't know where we were. So we asked people. There was a big water, like lake or bay or something. It turned out that was Lake Ontario. We were in a military camp called Fort Ontario. It has a national status, Fort Ontario, as a place where they used to fight British in another war. Anyway, and we were inside. Depending on what color we were, that was our barrack. And we were assigned to the barrack number. And we were dispersed. Later, I will show you some of the people. They're floating around getting their numbers squared away. Anyway, for a whole month we could not go out. And the people of Oswego could not come in. They were gathered at the fence. They were welcoming. There were wonderful people. We never saw anything like that. They would throw stuff to us, toys, clothes. They would even throw bread. And they would say, "See this? This is real bread. You know, that's-- I bet you don't know what this is, bread made from real flour. I'll throw you a piece so you can eat it." They would throw over the fence. And that's how they keep us from starving-- no, not at all. They gave us good food. They had three kitchens, three military type kitchens. And they [COUGHING] prepared good meals. As soon as we came, they found people who can cook. And they took them for cooking.

And you have your hands up? No. And anyway, good food. I didn't care. But my parents and other grown-ups, they were saying, what's going on? We're going from one wire fence to another. But that was not to torture us. Within a month, the local people were allowed to come in. Oh, by the way, news people they did a lot of work on us. There was a Time Magazine came out, dated August 4th. If you go to your local library, you will see refugees arrive, you know? But anyway, there's a lot of writing and pictures, because news people were allowed in and out. Anyway, we were allowed to go to school. We were assigned to different schools in town. And I was assigned to sixth grade. By this time, I was 15 years old. And 15 in sixth grade was a little retarded. Anyway, [PAUSES] we would go out in the morning, sign out in the guards' barrack at the gate, and they would check us out.

And by

4:30, you were supposed to be back in. Otherwise, they send the guards all over town looking for you. So anyway, we were living that way. At one point, they gave us some money to buy clothes, about $15 per grown up. I was 15. I was a grown up. I got a job in the kitchen helping the cooks. I remember they were mean. I was a little guy, 15. And then made me scrub the big, I don't know what, cauldrons. If soup would burn, I would have to scrape the bottom and all that for $15 a month. Otherwise, I get $8 and a half. Anyway, people had some money, some. So we lived there for a year and a half. I will just say in this year and a half, I had gone from sixth grade to seventh, to eighth, and I was in ninth grade. And I didn't finish the ninth grade, but I had signed up. Anyway, we didn't know any English, but when you get in a group of people speaking just English, and they start picking on you, making fun, you'll learn the language very quickly. And so I did learn English very quickly. I might say language has come to me pretty quickly. Anyway, so we lived there, every day to school and back. And they-- people from outside were allowed to come in, sign in. And at the end of the day, they had to sign out. So life in camp was not only livable, but for me, it was very exciting and relaxing. What shall I say? If I was outside, I wouldn't do any better. So there came January 1946. They said, "OK, everybody out of the camp and go back to your own country." Well, first segment of people that went back to their own country were some people who had relatives in Italy or in Yugoslavia, and they had made contact.

We don't have that. We knew our sister was there. But while we were here at camp, she got married. So she had a house. And, I mean, she had her own home. So we didn't leave United States to go see her. We figure we'll bring her over. To jump two to three years later when we made a big effort to get her over, the question was, were you a communist? The guy was. Her husband was a communist. So that knocked her out. Yes. I saw your hand.

INT: Yes. I wanted to ask you while you were in the camp in Oswego, how much did you know about the political conversation going on beyond the fence?

ID: OK.

Here is the thing. People of Oswego, they only knew English. People in the camp, they spoke Serbo-Croatian, and German, and Italian. There were three primary languages. And it didn't help us for conversation and communication. But we started a little bit here and there.

The people in school, I went to a junior high school, some made fun of us. Even some teachers made fun of us. They told us, excuse me, but, you know, the situation is funny. And we learned English so rapidly. When your life depends on it, when your teacher sends you downstairs to the office to get some [? electrons ?] and go down there and they're laughing. I said, "Why is that a funny story?" He says, "No, tell the teacher that we have only round one, but she wants square ones, that we only have square ones. But I know him. He wants round ones, [? electrons." ?] So we go there. Teachers, they don't have any round electron. They only have square ones Did they give you any? No. Do you want? No, I'll send somebody else. You know, stuff like that went on. And what was it? [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] Yeah, I forgot, a little anecdote, I had a whole slew where they tell you you're nuts. And you think they're giving you a compliment, the kids. But anyway, we learned the language. We advanced. And the war ended. My parents, it broke their heart. They have to go someplace. This camp has been good to them.

They didn't have any ambition or interest in anything. And they didn't learn any English, just expression here and there. So people like me, I want to go to school. I want to go to high school. Well, I'll just tell you. I am the only guy making a living. I am now 15 and 15 and 1/2. And let me go back. They-- yeah, the organization, they were organized. They decided that they will let people stay, because many have close relatives in the United States. And what's 1,000 in 66,000,000? That was the population in those days. Anyway, so they let us stay. But you have to go. Now, first it was, go back to your own country. And we say, which one is our own country? We don't have one. They chased us out of here. They chased us out of there. So that was impossible. So, OK, where do you want to go? Well, we'll go-- let's say we want to stay here in the United States. Finally, it was-- the law was amended, they can. But first, they have to leave the United States, and we'll give them visas, and then they can re-enter. OK. So that was resolved. And we were to leave United States and go to a place. [PAUSES] Every family had to choose where they were going. But this organization that sponsored us, there was, let's see, two Jewish organizations. I think maybe it was partially, but no. The one that now supports everybody, because we're Jewish. I'll think. This happened 83 years ago, so I'll think of organization. It's a code with the letters, four or five letters. Anyway, so they have representatives in each town. And they would like for us to touch base with where we're going, so they can sort of ramrod us, you know, take charge of us, make sure we get a job, go to school, get an apartment. So they asked us, and this was in English, people come there from the Jewish Federation, or one is Jewish Federation. The other one is-- [PAUSES] they are not so pro-Israel anymore. I'll think. Anyway, Jewish Federation wants us to go where they have a representative. So where? They say go to Dallas. We have people there that will help you. Dallas? Where is that? It says Dallas, Texas. My father nearly fainted. Texas? There you have cowboys and Indians. They will shoot me. Who's going to shoot you? Nobody knows. Oh, you don't know, cowboys and Indians. I've seen movies. OK, so no Dallas. Well, where do you want to go? To a big city. OK, Chicago. Oh, yeah, Al Capone. Aye, he shoots. He's got a big gang. So where? They suggest Philadelphia. "OK, my father says, "What is Philadelphia?" He thought something to eat, you know. He never heard of Philadelphia. So we were assigned to Philadelphia. And we were going to go to Canada first, and then from Canada go to Philadelphia. And this was our departure. They took us by trains to Niagara Falls. We were organized so many each day. We were organized. We crossed the border. At the border, they gave us a passport to enter Canada. Or was it another kind of gizmo? Anyway, we had a paper to go into Niagara Falls, Canada. It's a beautiful place, Niagara Falls. It's much nicer on the Canadian side than ours. But anyway, then we walked back. And at the middle point of a bridge, they gave us another visa. One was passport. One was visa. He says, welcome to United States, with our name, and picture, and all that. We came singing and dancing into United States. And my mother and father, and two sisters, and I, and we were helped to board trains to go to Philadelphia. We went to Philadelphia. A guy met us from the Jewish Federation. He showed us where he had found an apartment for us, third floor, nothing very spacious, nothing very luxurious. But anyway, I was 15 and 1/2. He found me a job. The rest, my father couldn't speak English, my mother also. My two sisters were too young to work. So they had to go sign up for school. So [PAUSES] that's what happened. They signed up to the local school, number 2, or whatever it was, in Philadelphia. And I didn't go to school for half a year. And I got a job in a hat factory. Now, some of you may not know what a hat is. But you wear it on top of your head. And people in those days, they all wore hats. Men wore hats and women wore them for high fashion. But men wore them for display. You take off your hat to somebody if you want to say hello. And I started making money for the family, $14.50. It started with 16, is my-- $0.45 an hour. I was getting that money, handing it to my father who would ration the food to us, and that was the worst part of my life. And then I advanced in this factory. And I got to the point where I was making a decent salary between overtime and extra jobs on weekends. I was really helping the family. Anyway, so I was making, I think dollar and a half, and now dollar and a quarter. Anyway, I went to school. I graduated high school with cap and gown at night. It was very, very solemn for me. And then I went to college, Temple University, at night again. And I finished my college education. And between here and there, I decided it was time for me to get out of the factory and go into normal jobs. And one girl that I was dating, she worked for a guy that sold chickens, killed chickens. And they were packaged in crates, and they were shipped or carried, I guess, by truck to different, what you call it, like Giants, or Safeway, or something, places like that.

INT: Grocery stores.

ID: Or even in some other towns to sell retail. And I worked in that. I was the accountant and payroll person. And I felt so big because I was out of the factory and in the-- well, a place that was quasi office. And I was getting $60 an hour, and that went OK. And then I found a job at Minneapolis, Honeywell. And that was my good job, office job. Yes?

INT: I'm sorry. But did you serve in the military?

ID: Do what?

