ID: Oh, that from Honeywell, there's when they said we are fighting a war here, and you're just the man for us. So I went to the-- what is it? Board, selection board. And I ended up going into the military. And I was sent to basic training in South Carolina, Jackson, South Carolina. I'm sorry-- Columbia, South Carolina. Fort Jackson was the military camp. And I was there for eight, nine weeks. Eight weeks, but one week before we started, so nine weeks. And they said, you know what? We'll give you a week, what they call it? Vacation, they didn't call it vacation. They had a name for it. And then you come back here. And I was in the heavy weapons unit. And they trained me for heavy weapons. I could fire several of the heavy weapons. And some I knew from the partisan days. And I was just-- I wasn't the shortest man in the army, but they had some short ones.

INT: What do you mean by heavy weapons? Can you give us some examples?

ID: Height, I was--

INT: No, no heavy-- you said you work with heavy weapons.

ID: Heavy weapons.

INT: Yeah, can you give us examples of what kinds of weapons?

ID: Artillery, and see they had mortars that you carry and mortars that were on wheels. So artillery mortars is 106 was the number. And what else they had? Before was the light machine guns and the riflemen. You're basically a rifleman in the army. But machine gun was the heaviest. And two guys carried a tube where you would drop the weapon, whoosh, depending on how much charge you put and that's how high it would go, turn around, and then explode. That's for infantry. But ours was really throwing at a distance. And artillery is cannons and some heavy, let's see, there was 106 millimeter howitzer. That meant you have a little thing here. You adjust it, and then you put in the big projectile. And it goes where you adjusted it to. We learned how to fire those too. And we learned how to go at night, how to get lost and unlost. And, you know, I was going to say, you know, like my mother used to say. You know, you've been a soldier. Well, there was artillery, not the heaviest gun, but it was pretty heavy guns. And they called that-- they called this recoil-less rifle. And the rifle is what you carry on the shoulder. But this was two very mean piece of equipment. We saw. We fired them at the things and up even sand and embankment. Anyway, so we were trained for that. And the next eight weeks, I spent in the light artillery and heavy weapons infantry. And I wanted to know what does heavy weapon infantry do. And they told me when this sniper where the guy sees something and it's motorized, he calls for assistance, and they fire this recoil-less rifle. Anyway, [PAUSES] then they send us home again for leave. That's what it is, military leave. And then you report at the draft board. And they have your orders. And my orders sent me to France. [PAUSES] And I went. [PAUSES] When my time came, I went with the ticket. They give you a railroad ticket, airplane ticket, whatever you need. They schedule you, and you're supposed to go to Jackson, and then Jackson gives you more airline tickets, and more train tickets. And you go from New York Harbor. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] I was on the leave. I remember I had taken leave, which I shouldn't have. I was given-- while waiting in New Jersey for my ship to be ready for boarding, they gave me all kinds of duties, cleaning up sergeants' living quarters, and barracks, and all that. And I didn't care for that. So there came this May, end of May was a weekend. I forgot what is this holiday. I went to Philadelphia. I stayed there until Monday. And then my ship, my group, had gone on the ship and ship was ready for departure. They drove me by PM police-- MP. MP drove me to the ship, put me there, and I was supposed to be under company commander's 14 days observation and special details. Well-- not captain, under sergeant. Well, it turned out sergeant played cards, Pinochle. I used to like to play Pinochle. So we got together. There were some other people playing Pinochle. And I served my special duty playing Pinochle with one sergeant and one of the other civilians. Anyway, and our ship went back to Europe. And it went to Bremerhaven. That's north, due north of Germany. That's where German port was. I guess they have other ports, but this was where the Allies sent supplies. So I'm shipped down from-- that ship sent me down toward France, and nobody giving me tickets, nothing. Anyway, I was given the job as a busboy in an officers' what they call? Canteen or something where the officers eat. And the officers would sometimes be served with German waitresses, and somebody leaves a tip. And I said, hey, where's my tip? I'm bussing. And then I kept some tips, not much. Anyway, I said, I can't stand being among Germans. Although by that time, I had learned a few words in German. And I could keep a conversation. Anyway, then after a while, I talked to somebody. They put me on a train. I went to France. We got to this place, a God-forsaken place, nothing but humps inside the-- these are petroleum inside these humps. They were like a Quonset hut or something. And one of these was for us to live. So we were living there in the Quonset huts or some in barracks. I got a place in barracks. And I'd like to tell you a story that I only tell to my friends. [PAUSES] By that time, by the time I got to France, the captain says, "Huh, you're my interpreter." I said, "In what language?" He says, "French, of course." I said, "Captain, I don't speak a word of French. He says, "You're a translator, and when I need translating, you're going to come and translate. Is that clear?" I said, "Yes sir." He calls me back. "Go to the company clerk. Let him give you some money and buy yourself a book. When I need you, I want you up here on the double speaking French." So he gave me money to buy a book, like $5.50, Michelin. Yeah. And I was-- I was given the time off. I had a duty, but I was given other time, and a Jeep. I go to the beach, spread my beach blanket. I have my portable radio and I start studying. Within 10 minutes, I'm surrounded by young French people of both denomination, male and female. And they talked to me. And I talked back. Six weeks comes, captain calls me. "There's an epidemic of flu in these towns here, and I don't want my soldiers to catch flu. We're going to go and talk to mayors of these towns. And you're my interpreter? And you know what? We took captain's Jeep. He was the head of our unit-- his Jeep, and a colonel, head of the hospital, and the chief nurse. She was major something or other. We made fun of her after that movie came out. Yeah. And we went from little towns from one to the other, and I translated. And the captain said to me, "Sonny, you got yourself out of a brig." OK.

