INT: Before we conclude, I have one or two additional questions. [PAUSES] Do you know how your mother and your sisters, the younger ones and the older one, how they survived?

ID: Yes, I do.

INT: Could you tell us briefly?

ID: Sure. Two sisters and my mother, they were living in Split until a friend said that she heard that by end of January, there won't be a single living Jewish person in Split. [PAUSES] Let me take a drink. [DRINKING]

Well, that was pretty scary. That's when my sister-- and she was at that time, let me see. That was '43, and she's-- what was she? 13? Yeah, just about about-- no less than 13. She took control of my mother, who was very afraid of everything. And the little girl, and she says, "Mom, we're going to the milk lady." "But will she take us? Won't she? How will I go? What will I do? We have a store there. We have an apartment." "Mom, let's save our lives. Go your milk lady." So they went to the milk lady in nearby village. Now Split is surrounded by umpteen villages. So they went there, and the milk lady took them. They gave them living quarters in a closet.

My sister describes the closet, 3 and 1/2 by 6. She says they put-- she put them there because that's all she had. And they locked themselves in. Well, that was away from where the Germans were. But she wasn't supposed to make sound. And the Germans would come occasionally with their Jeeps. They didn't call them Jeeps-- their motorcycles and attachment. And they would ask where they can buy wine, where they can find social activity, whatever. And the milk lady would shrug her shoulder. And then one time, she says to my mother and older sister, "I'm sorry. I did all I could. You have to leave. If they detect you here, it's you and me that are going to lose their heads." You know German, whoop. And my sister-- oh, by the way, my sister was crying before. "Mother, let's go. Let's go." "Where am I going to go?" She says to the milk lady, "Well, how do I? How will I survive, all that? I can't walk that much." Well, anyway, my sister says, "OK, I'm leaving. Goodbye, Mom." And she started leaving. She took the little girl with her and waved to my mother. And my mother said, "Wait, I'm coming with you." OK. So they-- that's how they went to the village. And the milk lady took them in. And after that milk lady says, "I can't. I like to keep you, but it's too dangerous." So the husband says, her brother-in-law in another village, somewhat distant from Split where we live, he may be able to hide her better. So they went there. They hid better there. And then the Germans were coming in all the islands, establishing their units, so they can come against British on the other island. So they chased them again out of there. And my mother and sisters were hopping from one island to another. And finally, they ended up on an island called Vis, V-I-S. And that was the unit-- unit? No, that was the single place where British would come with their PT boats or whatever. And they would unload sometimes supplies, and they had a group of people that was trying to get to Italy, the refugees. So they took them. They took my mother and sister, and they were going to live in camp. By that time, when they came, we had already lived in this city called Lecce for a while, and we were notified that my sister and my mother were coming just as we signed up to go to United States.

INT: You told us that.

ID: Yeah. So that's a connecting point. And we went and met them. And that's-- and I treated them to big ice cream called [? casati ?] and they got sick over that. They haven't eaten for many days.

